[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Seed dance 2.5 is finally here and so many people have been saying that this is the future of filmm. So in this video we\'re going to be taking a hands-on look at seed dance 2.5. I\'ll talk about some of the new features in this AI video'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model. We\'ll also talk about how it stacks up, what it costs, and what this means for the future of creativity. All right, let\'s get in. In order to use Cedance 2.5 at this time, you have to go through Dramino, which is the official'

[00:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aggregator of Bite Dance. So, we\'re here on the Dramino website and at the bottom you can see we have our menu here. In order to generate a video in Cedance 2.5, just make sure you\'re in the AI'

[00:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video section here. You can type in your prompt and you can bring in references. So, inside of Seed Dance, you can bring up to 50 references, which is a huge amount of references compared to what we'

[00:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'had with just the 2.0 version. For this example, we\'re going to use this image of this spaceship falling to Earth. And under the model, we\'ll make sure we\'re in Sea Dance 2.5. Now, in terms of'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'resolution, right now, you only have the ability to generate outputs in 720p. So, you\'re always going to need to use an AI upreser if you want to use these generations in a professional capacity.'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And you do have the option to pick from various aspect ratios, including 9x6 if you want to do social mediacentric content or something like micro dramas. And for the actual duration of the'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video, you can generate a clip up to 30 seconds long. And if you remember, the normal version of Cance allows you to do cuts between your shots in a single generation. So, hypothetically, you can'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do multiple cuts up to 30 seconds long in a single output. And we\'ll do some tests in just a little bit to see how well that stacks up. So, when you\'re ready, all you have to do is go ahead'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and hit generate. Now, real quick, let\'s take a look at a few of our examples. And I should note that they all have sound effects built in. So, this is our first shot here. And the ship blows up. The physics look really, really nice and'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it just plummets through the cloud. Obviously, the physics and the visual effects look incredible and it really mirrors what you would expect to see from a high budget visual effects shot. The problem is there is quite a bit of'

[02:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'artifacting inside of the video itself. It almost looks like a low compression version of a high budget movie. And so again, you would need to use an AI system to upres this if you wanted to'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use it on a professional project. We also have this shot here of a spaceship flying. And yeah, I mean, it just looks really good. Obviously, in this specific shot, I'

[02:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think there\'s some continuity issues with the clouds and where you would expect them in relation to the Earth, but the overall physics look very, very nice. We also took this image of this soldier here, and we wanted him to run'

[02:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into battle. And I mean the camera shake, the mud from his feet, it looks really, really good. Obviously, there\'s some physical problems, like the way in which the dirt kind of evaporates as opposed to hitting'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the ground there is a little off. And there is some vertical banding happening. So again, it\'s not perfect. Everyone online\'s going to say it\'s perfect. It is not, but it is very, very impressive. We also have this shot here'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where we wanted to prompt for him running into battle. And I mean, yeah, it\'s super super impressive. So, now that we\'ve taken a look at a few shots and examples from Cedance 2.5, now let\'s'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get into the individual features and see how they stack up. So, for our first example, I wanted to do this alien invasion example where we prompted for a guy who\'s like running from an alien and'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then they land at the very end and you know something bad is going to happen. So, we have a few different references here. We have this image of a spaceship. We have this small town. I liked this image a lot for color grading. We have'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this alien named Jerry here. And uh Tyler, we may have to blur that out for the folks. And we were very specific about what we wanted to see from the overall scene. And from seed dance, we got this'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fight. We shot Terren.'

[04:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; I don\'t know what he said there at the end. Okay, so wildly impressive. Obviously, Seed Dance, there\'s something intellectually that\'s not happening with Seed Dance. Uh, for example, the bus,'

[04:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the way it flips was unmotivated, so you\'d probably have to cut around that as an editor. And, you know, the guy obviously looking the wrong direction compared to where the spaceship is flying is a little funny, but it\'s pretty wild. Like, the continuity with'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the character throughout a 30 secondond scene. remember just a few years ago generating a shot that was 5 seconds long was a huge accomplishment. The fact that it\'s able to pull off a 30-cond scene and the continuity looks so good'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is frankly really, really impressive. Now, we wanted to do the same thing for an advertising project. So, I have this image of me as a character sheet that we brought into Seed Dance. And we have'

[05:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this shot of Shelby, who\'s my wife, although I think her face begins to change there towards the end, but you know what? We\'re just going to roll with it. and we prompted for an entire 30- secondond ad. I\'m not going to read you out the entire prompt, but you can learn'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more by clicking the link below this video. And that gave us this Oh, amazing. Amazing. So, it\'s so funny'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because even though in the prompt everything totally made sense, you can see how Seed Dance really doesn\'t intelligently understand what\'s happening. There\'s just so many weird quirks with the way in which the'

[06:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'characters interact and like the cups having lights in them there. There\'s definitely some stuff that is lost with Seed Dance. So, this is by no means type in a prompt, get a full advertisement in return. You\'re going to need to prompt'

[06:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it, you\'re going to need to direct it, and it\'s going to take iteration to get where you want to go. But again, that was all from a single prompt for that entire 32nd generation, which is frankly very impressive. So, the quick takeaway'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is 32nd generations are impressive, but they\'re not going to be production or advertisement ready. So, you probably need to break them up into smaller clips. Anyways, for our next example, we wanted to really push this idea of'

[07:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'creating a single long shot from a single generation. And so, we have the same reference image of me, and we wanted to put me in this suburban environment. And basically, I\'m talking'

[07:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the camera for 30 seconds as shenanigans unfold. Let\'s take a look. &gt;&gt; Yeah. Does 2:00 still work for you? I can push it to 2:30 if you need. &gt;&gt; Hey, did you get a chance to send over'

[07:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that file? I still don\'t see it in my inbox. &gt;&gt; Honestly, the Wi-Fi at the office was so slow today, I couldn\'t even load a page. Anyway, let me know if the 230 works.'

[08:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, talk later. &gt;&gt; Okay, that\'s some really impressive match action there, but we prompted for the entire scene to be a single shot and it really uh took some creative'

[08:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'liberties there. So, again, it\'s pretty wild that it was able to match the character and create that matchaction effect. We actually featured a film recently that took that same concept uh in an advertising capacity and did an'

[08:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'amazing job. But again, you\'re getting into some logic loss whenever you try to generate those 30 secondond outputs. It\'s very impressive, but it\'s just worth noting. Next up, we wanted to test out using a lot of the reference images.'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The idea is that you can upload a lot of assets. It could be your characters, it can be their clothing, it could be small objects that are inside the overall environment, and then you also have the ability to upload reference video and'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'audio as well. And so we have a bunch of different items here. And I basically wanted to create an entire scene that\'s happening inside an antiques market. And we have everything from the main'

[09:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character to a supporting character to the shop owner himself and just different items that we want to see inside of this French market film that we\'re working on. So, we brought all of'

[09:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'those references together, wrote a very long prompt of what we wanted to see, and this is what we got. Okay. I don\'t know why he turned into'

[09:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the green lantern at the end there. We did not prompt for that. So, it is impressive. It\'s definitely including a lot of the items that we uploaded. You can see we have the trumpet there. We have the sextant. We have the final glass that we want him to pick up, but'

[10:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s also taking some creative liberties. For example, the fox was not inside of the glass case. and the pocket watches were weirdly connected by a single chain. But one thing I really like about the generation, in addition to the physics being really impressive,'

[10:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the fact that it brought in all of the characters that we uploaded, but it didn\'t make them main characters. It put them correctly inside of the environment. For example, we have our main character and he looks fine, but we also have the shopkeeper, but he\'s a'

[10:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little out of focus in the background, but there and it feels realistic. And we also have our background actor in the red coat and she\'s completely out of focus, but she\'s present in the scene which helps with the overall realism of'

[10:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the piece. Now, one thing I do want to point out, this definitely feels like a.5 improvement over Seed Dance 2 and not a complete overhaul of the model. Some of the main problems that I\'m'

[11:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looking at are some jitteriness to the motion. It\'s almost like the shutter speed changes throughout the overall scene, which we saw in previous versions of seed dance. You also have a lot of kind of bakedin artifacting that feels'

[11:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like it\'s baked into the model in the outputs that are generated. I\'m not entirely sure why. Google VO also struggles with the same problem, but it\'s even more apparent in the 2.5 version of Seedance. The next thing I'

[11:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to look at is how Cance 2.5 stacks up against other AI video models. They said it\'s a big improvement. So, let\'s take a look. So, I have this shot here of this man riding a moped and we'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'animated him inside of Seedance 2.5 and that gave us this. &gt;&gt; Hello, my friends.'

[11:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, it did fine. I can\'t tell if it\'s in a slow frame rate or live action. It\'s a little unclear here. But let\'s'

[12:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take a look at the same generation inside of Cling. Well, hello my friends.'

[12:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; So, the lip syncing is nowhere close and a lot of those background characters are just like morphing and weird. So, I feel like Cance 2.5 did a much better job than Cling. Now, let\'s compare it'

[12:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'against Cance 2.0. &gt;&gt; Hello, my friends. Okay, so again, it\'s not perfect, but I'

[12:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually think in this test 2.0 did a better job than 2.5. Now, the one test that I was crazy excited to run is the asteroid test. So, we have this shot of a truck driving away from these'

[13:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'asteroids. We wanted a prompt for an entire scene where they come crashing down. And we use C dance 2.5. And that gave us this. Okay, it\'s fine. Not the most dynamic'

[13:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'physics, but the particles, the way that the background explosion happens is very cool. But the reason why I\'m saying it\'s okay is because the previous version of Sea Dance gave us this, which I mean, come on, is still amazing.'

[14:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The next test I want to show you is a multi-shot scene. So, we have this image here of a woman in a diner and we prompted for a whole scene where a guy comes in, sits down, takes a bite of a burger, and you\'ll see in just a second.'

[14:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, inside of Cance 2.5, we got this. Thanks for the food. I\'m starving.'

[14:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; I\'m sorry. who are you? &gt;&gt; Okay, so there\'s some issues with continuity and like the neon signs don\'t really make a lot of sense, but the final two shots there of the guy sitting'

[14:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'down and then cutting to the woman looks incredibly realistic. Now again, if we compare that to the previous version of Sea Dance, we got this. &gt;&gt; Thanks for the food. I\'m starving.'

[15:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m sorry. Who are you? &gt;&gt; Which again, that scene is not bad. I do think that the skin in 2.0 is a little more plasticky compared to the skin outputs that we\'re getting from 2.5, at least up to this point. So, next up, I'

[15:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really wanted to test out the emotional resonance of Seed Dance 2.5. The idea is, can it actually give us a performance that can go past the uncanny valley? And so, for this example here,'

[15:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we have a few different shots. So, we have me and Shelby, and we wanted to have a really emotional scene inside of this coffee shop. So, let\'s take a look at our output.'

[15:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; I\'ve been trying to figure out how to say this since Thursday. &gt;&gt; I know.'

[15:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ve known since March. &gt;&gt; March. Why didn\'t you? &gt;&gt; Because I kept thinking you\'d tell me. &gt;&gt; I was going to.'

[16:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Okay, so you get the idea. You can feel a lot of emotional weight behind those generations. I think that anyone says that AI is not quite there yet or it\'s just really uncanny really ultimately'

[16:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'isn\'t curating the quality of the outputs that they\'re consuming because it absolutely is. Now, that doesn\'t mean that these shots are perfect. There\'s definitely some issues, especially with text in the final outputs, but the fact'

[16:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the matter is it\'s very emotionally resonant, and 2.5 is a really good improvement. Next up, by using Cance 2.5, you can generate a clip up to 3 minutes long by slowly continuing to'

[16:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extend your output. So, it\'s only 30 seconds at a time, but it can be up to 3 minutes long. So, for this example, we wanted to have some fun with it. So, we uploaded the stock reference video from Art List and had Seed Dance 2.5 continue'

[16:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to generate the scene. And there\'s some parts that are pretty funny. Obviously, the text in it is really weird and it just gets really wonky. The guy also begins speaking Chinese, which makes'

[17:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sense because I would imagine the majority of the training data is in Chinese, but it also goes completely off the rails there towards the end, which is uh kind of awesome. Like I think it\'d be really fun to watch these infinite'

[17:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'canvas films that just get really wonky over time. Uh so it\'s not producing anything that I would say would resonate with an audience again unless you curate the outputs. Now in terms of overall cost, believe it or not, Cance 2.5 is'

[17:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not a free video model. So on the Dino website, you can generate in 480p or 720p. I\'m going to focus on 720 because that\'s just as close as we can get to'

[17:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the professional resolution that we\'d be looking for. So, if you generate a 5-second clip at 720p, it\'s going to cost you $218. And if you generate one of those'

[17:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '30-second shots that I showed you, it\'s going to be $1310. And while I think it\'s very impressive that you can generate a video clip up to 30 seconds long, the fact of the matter is in none of the generations that we'

[18:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'saw would you actually be able to use it on a project because there were problems. So you\'re probably going to want to break it up anyways. So in terms of how that stacks up against 2.0, Cance 2.0 would cost you $114 for 5 seconds in 720p versus $2.18'

[18:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for 2.5. So it\'s almost twice as expensive. So, the big question is, is it twice as good? And the short answer is no. It\'s not twice as good. And you don\'t have the ability to generate in 4K'

[18:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or 1080p. So, you\'re going to have to add an additional amount of cost associated to your production because you\'re going to have to use an AI video upreser like Topaz in order to get it into a higher resolution format. The big'

[18:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'value in using Seedance 2.5, in my opinion, is the expanded reference assets that you can upload. Having the ability to bring in more videos and more images gives you more control over your'

[19:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'outputs. Obviously, from this video, you can see that those outputs aren\'t always exactly what you\'re looking for, but having a larger context window is absolutely the future of production, and I think it\'s a great improvement. So,'

[19:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the quick summary is from a physical dynamics and emotional resonance perspective, Cance 2.5 looks like it\'s the best video model on the market. But from a price and fidelity perspective,'

[19:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m not entirely convinced it\'s the best video model for most projects, but I\'m sure you\'re going to see a thousand influencer posts that say the opposite. Thank you so much for watching this video. We\'ll continue to test Cedance'

[19:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2.5 to see all of the interesting workflows that we can pull off with the tool. But in the meantime, if you want even more honest reviews and workflows in the latest AI tools, be sure to like and subscribe here on YouTube. Thank you'

[19:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so much for watching. Let me know what you think of Seedance 2.5 in the comments.'
